Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book by Margery Williams Bianco
Draft date: 3/20/2025
Notes: Medium = photorealistic live-action period children's drama; naturalistic early-20th-century English nursery, garden, and woodland; humans and animals share one photoreal live-action medium throughout.

FADE IN:

EXT. BOY'S STOCKING - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A plump Christmas stocking hangs in bright festive light. Wedged at the top sits the VELVETEEN RABBIT: fat and bunchy, spotted brown-and-white velveteen coat fully intact, real thread whiskers, ears lined with crisp pink sateen, polished boot-button eyes, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: soft rustle of tissue paper, distant cheerful voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Below him in the stocking: nuts, oranges, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, a clockwork mouse. For at least two hours THE BOY, in soft flannel nightclothes, loves the Rabbit.

Then Aunts and Uncles arrive. Tissue paper rustles. Parcels unwrap. In the excitement the Velveteen Rabbit is forgotten on the nursery floor.

(SOUND: laughter, paper tearing, footsteps receding)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Warm early-20th-century English nursery: wooden floors, hearth with fender, toy cupboard, mantel. The Velveteen Rabbit, still mostly new but slightly dusty, lies ignored on the floor or in the toy cupboard among superior toys.

(SOUND: distant nursery clock tick, soft toy clinks)

Mechanical toys look down on him. A model boat refers to its rigging. TIMOTHY, the jointed wooden lion, puts on airs. The Rabbit feels insignificant. Only the SKIN HORSE is kind: very old, brown coat bald in patches showing seams, most tail hairs pulled out, wise gentle eyes.

They lie side by side near the nursery fender.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

The Skin Horse smiles with quiet certainty.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, longing yet dreading the shabbiness.

(SOUND: Nana's approaching footsteps outside)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, in plain dark dress and white apron, rules the nursery. She swoops like a great wind tidying toys into the cupboard. THE BOY, in plain nightshirt, cannot find his china dog at bedtime. Nana is in a hurry. The toy cupboard door stands open.

(SOUND: cupboard door creak, brisk apron rustle)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms.

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

The Velveteen Rabbit sleeps in the Boy's bed. The Boy hugs him very tight, sometimes rolls over on him, sometimes pushes him far under the pillow. Night-light burns on the mantelpiece.

(SOUND: soft bedclothes rustle, child's steady breathing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

Soon the Rabbit grows to like it. The Boy talks to him, makes nice tunnels under the bedclothes like real rabbit burrows. They have splendid games in whispers. When the Boy sleeps, the Rabbit snuggles under his warm chin, the Boy's hands clasped round him all night.

Time passes. The Rabbit's beautiful velveteen fur grows shabbier, tail becoming unsewn, pink rubbed off his nose where the Boy has kissed him. He is so happy he never notices.

(SOUND: whispered child laughter fading to quiet night)

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Long spring days. THE BOY, in light outdoor play clothes, takes the Velveteen Rabbit everywhere: rides in the wheelbarrow, picnics on the grass, lovely fairy huts under the raspberry canes behind the flower border. The Rabbit's coat shows beginning wear, earth stains from flower-bed burrows.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow squeak, children's distant play)

One evening the Boy is called away suddenly. The Rabbit is left out on the lawn until long after dusk, wet through with dew and earthy. NANA comes with a candle, grumbling as she rubs him with a corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

The Boy sits up in bed and stretches out his hands.

(SOUND: bed creak, night-light hiss)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The little Rabbit hears. He knows the nursery magic has happened. He is Real. That night he is almost too happy to sleep. Love stirs in his sawdust heart. Into his dull boot-button eyes comes a look of wisdom and beauty.

NANA
(picking him up next morning)
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Near the house a wood. Long June evenings. THE BOY, grass-stained outdoor clothes, makes the Velveteen Rabbit a little nest among the bracken, then wanders to pick flowers or play at brigands. The Rabbit, noticeably loved-worn, fur thinner, spots fading, lies watching ants run between his velvet paws.

(SOUND: evening birds, soft bracken rustle, ants in grass)

Two strange beings creep out of the tall bracken: WILD RABBITS, quite furry and brand-new, seams not showing, bodies changing shape as they move, feet padding softly, noses twitching.

WILD RABBIT 1
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT 1
It's as easy as anything.

He hops sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT 1
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT 2
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit has no hind legs; his back is made all in one piece like a pincushion. He sits still.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The wild rabbit stretches his neck and looks.

WILD RABBIT 2
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

He laughs. The Velveteen Rabbit insists he is sitting on them. The wild rabbit whirls and dances till the little Rabbit grows dizzy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

All the while he longs to dance; a funny new tickly feeling runs through him. The strange rabbit comes close; long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, jumps backwards.

WILD RABBIT 1
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past. With a stamp of feet and flash of white tails the wild rabbits disappear into the bracken.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Only ants and gently swaying bracken. The Boy carries him home.

(SOUND: fading hops, bracken sway, approaching child footsteps)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

Weeks pass. The Velveteen Rabbit is very old and shabby: whiskers loved off, pink ear lining grey, brown spots faded, shape scarcely rabbit-like except to the Boy. To the Boy he is always beautiful. Shabbiness does not matter because he is Real.

Then the Boy falls ill. His face flushes. He talks in his sleep. His little body burns so hot it burns the Rabbit. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring, afraid someone might take him away. The Boy needs him.

(SOUND: feverish murmuring, footsteps of strangers, low all-night lamp hum)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a long weary time, for the Boy was too ill to play.

The Rabbit snuggles patiently, whispering plans of garden games and the raspberry thicket. Presently the fever turns. The Boy sits up and looks at picture-books, the Rabbit close at his side.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Bright sunny morning. Windows stand wide open. They have carried THE BOY, wrapped in a shawl, out onto the balcony. The little Rabbit lies tangled among the bedclothes, head peeping out, listening.

(SOUND: open-window breeze, distant garden birds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow. The room was to be disinfected, and all the books and toys that the Boy had played with in bed must be burnt.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
(brisk, dark suit, decisive)
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs!—Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

EXT. END OF THE GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

The little Rabbit is put into a sack with old picture-books and a lot of rubbish, carried to the end of the garden behind the fowl-house, a fine place for a bonfire. The gardener is busy with potatoes and peas; he promises to burn the lot next morning.

(SOUND: sack cloth drag, distant fowl cluck, garden tools)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

That night the Boy sleeps in a different bedroom with a splendid new white plush bunny with real glass eyes. He is too excited about the seaside to care much.

EXT. END OF THE GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

While the Boy dreams of the seaside, the little Rabbit lies among the old picture-books in the corner behind the fowl-house. The sack has been left untied. By wriggling he gets his head through the opening. He shivers; his coat is so thin and threadbare it is no protection. Nearby the thicket of raspberry canes grows tall and close like a tropical jungle.

(SOUND: night insects, distant house quiet, soft shivering cloth)

He remembers long sunlit hours, fairy huts, quiet evenings in the wood, the day he first knew he was Real, the wise Skin Horse. Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A tear, a real tear, trickles down his little shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: single soft drop on earth)

Where the tear fell a flower grows: slender green leaves the colour of emeralds, in the centre a blossom like a golden cup. It is so beautiful the Rabbit forgets to cry. The blossom opens. Out of it steps the NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY: dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers round her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower. She gathers him up and kisses him on his damp velveteen nose.

(MUSIC: soft luminous chime, rising gentle)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The Rabbit looks up; he seems to have seen her face before.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She holds him close and flies with him into the wood.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moon has risen. The forest is beautiful. Fronds of the bracken shine like frosted silver. In the open glade between tree-trunks the wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass. They stop and stand round in a ring to stare at the Fairy.

(SOUND: soft rabbit feet on grass, night breeze in leaves)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and puts him down on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits quite still, remembering his missing hind legs, too shy to move. He does not know that when the Fairy kissed him that last time she had changed him altogether. Something tickles his nose. Before he thinks he lifts his hind toe to scratch it.

He finds he actually has hind legs. Instead of dingy velveteen he has brown fur, soft and shiny. His ears twitch by themselves. His whiskers are so long they brush the grass. He gives one leap. The joy of using those hind legs is so great he goes springing about the turf, jumping sideways and whirling round as the others do. When at last he stops to look for the Fairy she has gone.

(SOUND: joyful rabbit thumps, grass swish, fading fairy chime)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Autumn and Winter pass. In the Spring, when the days grow warm and sunny, THE BOY, healthy again in outdoor play clothes, goes out to play in the wood behind the house. Two rabbits creep out from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through. About his little soft nose and his round black eyes there is something familiar.

(SOUND: spring birdsong, soft bracken stir, distant child footsteps)

THE BOY
(to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The marked rabbit watches a moment longer, then slips back into the bracken with his companion.

(MUSIC: gentle resolving theme, soft and warm)

FADE OUT.

THE END

